    return [
    [
        'slug'       => 'gift-tax-guide-2026',
        'title'      => 'Gift Tax 2026: Annual Exclusion and Lifetime Exemption',
        'keyword'    => 'gift tax',
        'desc'       => 'The 2026 gift tax annual exclusion is $19,000 per person, and the lifetime exemption is $15 million. See how it works and who actually pays it.',
        'excerpt'    => 'Most people who give money to family never owe a dollar of gift tax. Here is exactly why, and the few situations where it is worth paying attention.',
        'publish_at' => '2026-11-06 09:00',
        'answer'     => 'You can give up to $19,000 per person in 2026 without any gift tax paperwork at all. A married couple can give $38,000 to the same person. Go above that to one recipient and you owe no tax yet — you just file Form 709 and the excess is subtracted from a $15 million lifetime exemption. Almost nobody actually pays gift tax; the exemption is simply too large for most families to reach.',
        'takeaways'  => [
            'The 2026 annual gift tax exclusion is $19,000 per recipient, or $38,000 for a married couple splitting gifts.',
            'The lifetime gift and estate tax exemption is $15 million per person in 2026 — most people will never owe gift tax in their lifetime.',
            'Tuition and medical bills paid directly to the school or provider are gift-tax-free with no dollar limit.',
            'Giving above $19,000 to one person usually means filing a form, not paying a tax — the excess just reduces your lifetime exemption.',
        ],
        'content'    => '<p class="lead">If you searched for <strong>gift tax</strong>, you are probably trying to find out whether writing a check to your kid, helping with a down payment, or paying a grandchild\'s tuition is going to cost you anything. For the overwhelming majority of people, the answer is no. This federal tax exists, and it has real rules, but the exemptions are large enough that most Americans could give away a meaningful fortune and never owe a cent. This guide walks through the 2026 numbers, who actually has to think about this, and the handful of situations — large one-time gifts, 529 contributions, non-citizen spouses — where the details actually matter.</p>'
            . '<h2>The 2026 gift tax numbers at a glance</h2><table><thead><tr><th>Figure</th><th>2026 amount</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Annual exclusion, per recipient</td><td>$19,000</td></tr><tr><td>Annual exclusion, married couple splitting gifts</td><td>$38,000</td></tr><tr><td>Annual exclusion to a non-citizen spouse</td><td>$194,000</td></tr><tr><td>Lifetime gift and estate tax exemption, per person</td><td>$15,000,000</td></tr><tr><td>Form required above the annual exclusion</td><td>Form 709 (informational, usually no tax due)</td></tr><tr><td>Top gift tax rate (only once lifetime exemption is used up)</td><td>40%</td></tr></tbody></table><p>The lifetime exemption figure is new for 2026: the One Big Beautiful Bill Act made a $15 million exemption (indexed for inflation each year after) a permanent part of the tax code, replacing the lower figure that was previously scheduled to apply starting in 2026 under prior law. The 2027 annual exclusion has not been officially announced as of this writing — the IRS typically releases the next year\'s figure in the fall, so check IRS.gov before assuming it stays at $19,000.</p>'
            . '<h2>How much money can I gift tax free?</h2><p>In plain terms: up to $19,000 to any one person in 2026, as many separate people as you like, with zero paperwork and zero tax. Give your three adult children $19,000 each and that is $57,000 total, completely clean. If you are married, you and your spouse can combine your exclusions through "gift splitting," effectively doubling it to $38,000 per recipient, even if the money technically comes from one spouse\'s account — you just note the split on Form 709.</p><p>Go over $19,000 to one person in one year, and here is the part people get wrong: you do not suddenly owe tax on the excess. You file Form 709 to report it, and the excess amount is simply subtracted from your $15 million lifetime exemption. Only once your cumulative lifetime gifts (plus your taxable estate when you die) exceed $15 million does actual gift or estate tax come due, at rates up to 40%. For nearly every family reading this, that threshold is simply out of reach, which is exactly why most people who give generously to family never write a check to the IRS for it. If you are curious how a taxable estate above that threshold would actually be taxed, our <a href="{tool:estate-tax-calculator}">estate tax calculator</a> walks through the math.</p>'
            . '<h2>Who actually pays gift tax?</h2><p>The giver, not the receiver. If your parents give you $50,000 toward a <a href="/first-time-home-buyer-guide-2026/">down payment on a home</a>, you owe nothing and report nothing — any filing obligation and any eventual liability belongs entirely to them. This surprises people who assume a large gift shows up as income on their own tax return. It does not; gifts are never taxable income to the recipient under federal law.</p>'
            . '<h2>What is completely exempt, no matter the amount?</h2><ul><li><strong>Tuition paid directly to the school.</strong> Pay a university, private school or similar institution directly — not through the student — and there is no dollar limit and no exposure at all. Money handed to the student first, even if earmarked for tuition, does not qualify for this exemption and counts toward the annual exclusion instead.</li><li><strong>Medical expenses paid directly to the provider.</strong> Same rule: pay the hospital, doctor or insurer directly and it is unlimited and exempt. Reimbursing someone after they paid the bill does not count.</li><li><strong>Gifts to your U.S. citizen spouse.</strong> Unlimited and always tax-free, no form needed.</li><li><strong>Political contributions and most charitable gifts.</strong> These fall under separate rules entirely.</li></ul><p>This is why a grandparent who pays $40,000 a year directly to a private university bursar\'s office, every year, for a grandchild\'s entire K-12 and college education, can do so without touching the annual exclusion or the lifetime exemption at all — as long as the checks go straight to the school.</p>'
            . '<h2>The 529 plan superfunding trick</h2><p>If you want to front-load a child\'s <a href="/trump-account-vs-529/">529 college savings plan</a>, the IRS allows a special move: you can contribute up to five years\' worth of the annual exclusion in a single year — up to $95,000 in 2026 ($190,000 for a married couple splitting gifts) — and elect on Form 709 to treat it as if it were spread evenly over five years. Done this way, it uses none of your lifetime exemption, as long as you make no other gifts to that same beneficiary during the five-year window. This lets a lump sum, such as an inheritance or a bonus, get invested and compounding years earlier than five separate annual contributions would allow — the same early-compounding logic behind our guide on <a href="/how-to-save-10000-in-6-months-2026/">building savings quickly toward a goal</a>.</p>'
            . '<h2>Worked example: a family helping with a home down payment</h2><p>Two parents want to give their daughter and her husband $80,000 toward a house. Here is how it plays out under 2026 rules:</p><ul><li>Mother gives daughter $19,000 and son-in-law $19,000 — both inside the annual exclusion, no form.</li><li>Father does the same: $19,000 to daughter, $19,000 to son-in-law — also clean.</li><li>Total given: $76,000, completely gift-tax-free, no Form 709 required for any of the four separate gifts, because each one individually stayed at or under $19,000 per giver, per recipient.</li></ul><p>Notice how structuring the same $80,000 as four separate $19,000-or-under gifts, rather than one $80,000 lump sum from one parent, avoids any filing at all. This is a completely legal and common way families coordinate larger gifts.</p>'
            . '<h2>When do I actually need to file Form 709?</h2><p>File Form 709 for a calendar year if you gave any one person more than $19,000 (or $38,000 if you and your spouse elect to split gifts), made a 529 superfunding election, or gave a non-citizen spouse more than $194,000. Filing Form 709 almost never means writing a check to the IRS — it is primarily a record-keeping requirement that tracks how much of your $15 million lifetime exemption you have used, so the IRS and your estate have an accurate running total by the time you die. If you are coordinating a large year-end gift alongside other moves, our <a href="/year-end-tax-planning-checklist/">year-end tax planning checklist</a> covers the other December 31 deadlines worth checking at the same time.</p>'
            . '<h2>Does my state have its own gift tax?</h2><p>No U.S. state currently imposes a separate state-level version of this tax. A handful of states do have their own estate or inheritance tax, which applies after death and is a different matter entirely — that is a separate question worth checking with your state if you are doing broader estate planning, but it does not affect lifetime giving under the rules in this guide.</p>'
            . '<h2>Common mistakes people make</h2><ul><li><strong>Thinking tax is owed on every gift over $19,000.</strong> It is not — the excess reduces your lifetime exemption, and an actual bill is extremely rare.</li><li><strong>Handing a student cash for tuition instead of paying the school directly.</strong> This forfeits the unlimited tuition exemption and counts toward the annual exclusion instead.</li><li><strong>Not filing Form 709 when required.</strong> Even when nothing is owed, failing to file the form when you exceed the annual exclusion can complicate things for your estate later.</li><li><strong>Confusing this with income tax for the recipient.</strong> The person receiving a gift never owes income tax on it federally, no matter the size.</li><li><strong>Assuming next year\'s exclusion is the same as this year\'s.</strong> The IRS adjusts the annual exclusion periodically for inflation; always confirm the current year\'s figure on IRS.gov before relying on a specific dollar amount.</li></ul>'
            . '<h2>Gifting stock or other property, not just cash</h2><p>The rules above apply the same way to stock, a car, or any other property — the annual exclusion and lifetime exemption are measured by the fair market value of what you hand over, not just by cash. There is one detail worth knowing: when you give appreciated stock, the recipient generally takes over your original cost basis rather than the value on the day of the gift, per <a href="https://www.irs.gov/taxtopics/tc703" rel="noopener" target="_blank">IRS Topic 703</a>. That means if they eventually sell it, their capital gain is measured from what you originally paid, not from what it was worth when you gave it. For a highly appreciated stock, it can sometimes be more tax-efficient for the recipient if you sell it yourself and gift the cash instead, or hold it until death so they receive a stepped-up basis — a question worth running by a tax professional for a large transfer.</p>'
            . '<h2>Receiving a large gift from someone overseas</h2><p>If you are a U.S. person receiving more than $100,000 in a year from a foreign individual, or a smaller threshold from a foreign corporation or partnership, you generally have to report it to the IRS on <a href="https://www.irs.gov/forms-pubs/about-form-3520" rel="noopener" target="_blank">Form 3520</a>, even though you still owe no income tax on the money itself. This is purely an informational filing requirement, separate from everything above, and the penalties for not filing it when required can be steep — so it is worth checking if this applies to you before assuming a gift from family abroad needs no paperwork at all.</p>'
            . '<p>This guide is educational and not personal tax or legal advice. Questions about larger estates, non-citizen spouses, or trusts can get complex quickly — a CPA or estate attorney can confirm how these rules apply to your specific situation.</p>'
            . '<h2>A quick glossary</h2><ul><li><strong>Annual exclusion.</strong> The amount one person can give another in a calendar year with no form and no effect on the lifetime exemption — $19,000 in 2026.</li><li><strong>Lifetime exemption.</strong> The total amount a person can give away during life, plus leave at death, before any tax is actually owed — $15 million per person in 2026.</li><li><strong>Gift splitting.</strong> A married couple\'s election to treat gifts as coming half from each spouse, doubling the effective annual exclusion per recipient.</li><li><strong>Form 709.</strong> The informational return used to report gifts above the annual exclusion; filing it rarely means paying anything, since it mainly tracks how much of the lifetime exemption has been used.</li><li><strong>Stepped-up basis.</strong> The adjustment that resets an asset\'s cost basis to its value on the date of the owner\'s death, which is why holding appreciated property until death, rather than gifting it, can sometimes save the recipient capital gains tax later.</li></ul>'
            . '<h2>Sources</h2><p>IRS <a href="https://www.irs.gov/businesses/small-businesses-self-employed/frequently-asked-questions-on-gift-taxes" rel="noopener" target="_blank">frequently asked questions on gift taxes</a>; IRS <a href="https://www.irs.gov/forms-pubs/about-form-709" rel="noopener" target="_blank">About Form 709</a>; IRS <a href="https://www.irs.gov/taxtopics/tc703" rel="noopener" target="_blank">Topic 703, basis of assets</a>; IRS <a href="https://www.irs.gov/forms-pubs/about-form-3520" rel="noopener" target="_blank">About Form 3520</a>. The $15 million lifetime exemption and $19,000 annual exclusion for 2026 reflect the IRS\'s published inflation adjustments and the One Big Beautiful Bill Act; the 2027 annual exclusion had not been announced at the time of writing, so confirm the current figure on IRS.gov.</p>'
            . '{faq}',
        'faqs'       => [
            [ 'How much money can I gift tax free in 2026?', 'Up to $19,000 per person in 2026 with no form and no tax. A married couple can combine exclusions to give $38,000 per recipient through gift splitting, reported on Form 709 but typically owing no tax.' ],
            [ 'Do I have to pay taxes on a gift I receive?', 'No. Under federal law, the recipient of a gift never owes income tax on it, regardless of the amount. Any gift tax liability belongs to the person who gave the gift, not the person who received it.' ],
            [ 'What happens if I give someone more than $19,000 in a year?', 'You file Form 709 to report the gift, but you almost certainly will not owe any tax. The amount above $19,000 is simply subtracted from your $15 million lifetime gift and estate tax exemption — actual tax is only due once that entire lifetime exemption is used up.' ],
            [ 'Is there a lifetime gift tax exemption?', 'Yes — $15 million per individual in 2026, made a permanent, inflation-indexed part of the tax code by the One Big Beautiful Bill Act. Gifts above the $19,000 annual exclusion reduce this lifetime amount rather than triggering immediate tax.' ],
            [ 'Can I pay a grandchild\'s tuition without using my annual exclusion?', 'Yes, as long as you pay the school directly rather than giving the money to the student. Tuition paid directly to an educational institution is completely exempt from gift tax with no dollar limit, and the same applies to medical bills paid directly to the provider.' ],
            [ 'What is the gift tax rate?', 'If a taxpayer somehow exhausts the full $15 million lifetime exemption, the gift tax rate on amounts above it can reach 40%. In practice, this only affects very large estates — the overwhelming majority of people never pay gift tax at any rate.' ],
