    [
        'slug'       => 'how-long-will-my-money-last-in-retirement',
        'title'      => 'How Long Will My Money Last in Retirement?',
        'keyword'    => 'how long will my money last in retirement',
        'desc'       => 'See how many years your retirement savings could last at different withdrawal rates, why the 4% rule exists, and what drains savings faster than expected.',
        'excerpt'    => 'The honest answer depends on your withdrawal rate far more than most people expect. Here is the math, the 4% rule, and the risks a simple calculator cannot show you.',
        'publish_at' => '2026-11-06 21:00',
        'answer'     => 'A $500,000 portfolio withdrawing 4% a year ($20,000, rising with inflation) has historically had a high probability of lasting at least 30 years. Push that withdrawal to 6% ($30,000) on the same balance, and it can run dry in roughly 20 to 25 years depending on investment returns. The withdrawal rate, not the account size alone, is what mostly determines how long retirement savings last.',
        'takeaways'  => [
            'The traditional "4% rule" sets a first-year withdrawal at 4% of savings, adjusted for inflation each year after, aiming to last at least 30 years.',
            'A higher withdrawal rate shortens how long money lasts faster than most people expect — 6% instead of 4% can cut decades off the timeline.',
            'A few bad investment years early in retirement can deplete savings much faster than an "average return" projection suggests.',
            'Delaying Social Security, trimming spending in down years, and reviewing your withdrawal rate periodically all meaningfully extend how long savings last.',
        ],
        'content'    => '<p class="lead">"<strong>How long will my money last in retirement?</strong>" is one of the most searched questions in personal finance, and for good reason — it is the question that actually decides whether retirement feels secure or stressful. The honest, slightly uncomfortable answer is that it depends far more on how much you withdraw each year than on how much you saved in the first place. This guide walks through the math behind that answer, the famous "4% rule," and the risks that a simple average-return projection does not show you. You can run your own numbers in our <a href="{tool:retirement-withdrawal-calculator}">retirement withdrawal calculator</a>.</p>'
            . '<h2>The short version: it is mostly about your withdrawal rate</h2><p>Picture two retirees, each with exactly $500,000 saved. One withdraws $20,000 in the first year (a 4% withdrawal rate) and raises that amount with inflation every year after. The other withdraws $30,000 in the first year (a 6% rate) and does the same. Assuming similar investment returns, the first retiree\'s money can realistically last 30 years or more. The second retiree, despite starting with the identical balance, can run out in roughly half that time. The size of the nest egg matters, but the percentage you pull from it every year matters just as much, if not more.</p>'
            . '<h2>What is the 4% rule, and where did it come from?</h2><p>The 4% rule traces back to research from financial planner William Bengen in 1994, later reinforced by the widely cited 1998 "Trinity study." Both tested how a portfolio of U.S. stocks and bonds would have performed across every rolling 30-year period in historical market data, withdrawing a fixed percentage in year one and then adjusting that dollar amount upward each year for inflation. A 4% starting withdrawal rate, from a portfolio with roughly 50-75% in stocks, survived the vast majority of 30-year historical periods tested — including some brutal ones, like retirements that began right before major market downturns.</p><p>It is a rule of thumb built on the past, not a guarantee about the future. Some retirement researchers now argue for a more conservative 3% to 3.5% starting rate, particularly for people who retire early and need their money to last 40 years or more instead of 30, or who are skeptical that future market returns will match the historical average used in the original research. If you are pursuing early retirement specifically, our <a href="{tool:fire-calculator}">FIRE calculator</a> estimates how large a portfolio you would need to support a given withdrawal over a longer horizon.</p>'
            . '<h2>A side-by-side look at withdrawal rates</h2><table><thead><tr><th>Starting balance</th><th>Withdrawal rate</th><th>First-year withdrawal</th><th>Roughly how long it lasts*</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>$500,000</td><td>3%</td><td>$15,000</td><td>40+ years (very likely never depletes)</td></tr><tr><td>$500,000</td><td>4%</td><td>$20,000</td><td>~30 years (the classic benchmark)</td></tr><tr><td>$500,000</td><td>5%</td><td>$25,000</td><td>~25 years</td></tr><tr><td>$500,000</td><td>6%</td><td>$30,000</td><td>~20-25 years</td></tr><tr><td>$500,000</td><td>8%</td><td>$40,000</td><td>~15 years or less</td></tr></tbody></table><p>*Rough ranges assuming moderate investment growth and inflation-adjusted withdrawals; actual results vary significantly with real market returns, which never move in a straight line. Use the <a href="{tool:retirement-withdrawal-calculator}">retirement withdrawal calculator</a> to test your own balance, withdrawal amount, and return assumptions.</p>'
            . '<h2>The risk a simple calculator cannot fully show you: sequence of returns</h2><p>Here is the detail that trips up a lot of retirement planning: it is not just the average return over 30 years that matters, it is the order those returns happen in. Two retirees can experience the exact same average annual return over three decades and end up with wildly different outcomes, purely because of when the good and bad years happened.</p><p>A retiree who hits a market downturn in the first few years of retirement, while still withdrawing money every year, locks in losses on a shrinking balance — there is no time for a later recovery to help money that has already been spent. A retiree who experiences the identical downturn later in retirement, after years of growth built a larger cushion, weathers it far more easily. This is called sequence-of-returns risk, and it is the single biggest reason why two people who "saved the same amount" and "expect the same average return" can have very different retirements.</p>'
            . '<h2>What actually makes savings last longer</h2><ul><li><strong>Lower your withdrawal rate if you can.</strong> As the table above shows, the jump from 6% to 4% is often the difference between money running out in your 80s and never running out at all.</li><li><strong>Delay Social Security if it makes sense for you.</strong> Waiting past full retirement age adds roughly 8% a year to your benefit, up until age 70 — a meaningful, guaranteed increase that reduces how much you need to pull from savings for the rest of your life.</li><li><strong>Stay flexible in down markets.</strong> Retirees who trim discretionary spending after a bad market year, rather than withdrawing the same inflation-adjusted amount regardless of performance, have historically made their savings last meaningfully longer than a rigid fixed withdrawal.</li><li><strong>Keep some growth exposure.</strong> Moving entirely to cash feels safe but has historically failed to keep pace with inflation over a 20-30 year retirement, which can erode purchasing power just as surely as overspending does.</li><li><strong>Revisit the plan periodically, not just once.</strong> A withdrawal strategy set at age 65 should be reviewed every few years against how the portfolio has actually performed, not left on autopilot for three decades.</li></ul>'
            . '<h2>What this kind of estimate leaves out</h2><p>Any withdrawal-longevity estimate, including the calculator on this page, is a simplified model. It typically does not account for taxes on withdrawals (see our <a href="/roth-conversion-before-year-end/">Roth conversion guide</a> for one way to manage this ahead of retirement), Social Security income that can reduce how much you need from savings, required minimum distributions once you reach your 70s, healthcare cost spikes, or the common pattern of retirees spending more in active early "go-go" years and less in later ones. Treat any such number as a rough benchmark for comparison, not a complete, personalized retirement plan — a fee-only financial planner can run a more detailed analysis that accounts for your full picture, including how this fits with your broader <a href="/retirement-planning-how-much-need-2026/">retirement savings target</a>.</p>'
            . '<h2>Portfolio mix matters alongside the withdrawal rate</h2><p>How a portfolio is invested changes how long it is likely to last at a given withdrawal rate, not just how fast it grows. A portfolio that is entirely bonds and cash is less volatile year to year, but has historically struggled to keep up with inflation over a 20-to-30-year retirement, which can quietly erode how much a fixed-dollar withdrawal is actually worth over time. A portfolio with a meaningful allocation to stocks has historically supported higher sustainable withdrawal rates over long periods, precisely because growth, not just stability, is part of what makes savings outlast a multi-decade retirement — the trade-off is tolerating more short-term ups and downs along the way.</p>'
            . '<h2>Common mistakes people make with this question</h2><ul><li><strong>Fixating on the total balance and ignoring the withdrawal rate.</strong> $1 million withdrawn at 8% a year can run out faster than $400,000 withdrawn at 3%.</li><li><strong>Assuming a smooth, average return every year.</strong> Markets do not move in a straight line, and the timing of downturns matters as much as their size.</li><li><strong>Never adjusting spending after a bad market year.</strong> A rigid withdrawal plan that ignores portfolio performance increases the risk of running out early.</li><li><strong>Forgetting Social Security and other income in the picture.</strong> This type of calculator usually models savings withdrawals alone — if a pension or Social Security covers part of your spending, your real savings withdrawal need may be lower than your full budget.</li><li><strong>Treating the 4% rule as a precise, guaranteed number.</strong> It is a historically grounded benchmark, not a promise — your own situation, time horizon and risk tolerance may call for a different starting point.</li></ul>'
            . '<h2>Why delaying Social Security changes the whole picture</h2><p>According to the Social Security Administration, waiting past your full retirement age to claim benefits adds roughly 8% to your monthly benefit for every year you delay, up until age 70. For someone whose full retirement age is 67, claiming at 70 instead means a benefit about 24% larger than claiming at 67, and significantly larger than claiming early at 62. A bigger guaranteed, inflation-protected Social Security check directly lowers how much you need to pull from savings every single year for the rest of your life — which is why some retirees choose to draw down savings a bit faster in their 60s specifically to delay claiming, then lean more heavily on Social Security once it starts. See our guide to the <a href="/average-social-security-check-by-age/">average Social Security check by age</a> to compare claiming ages against typical benefit amounts.</p>'
            . '<h2>A 2026 worked example: combining savings and Social Security</h2><p>Consider a 65-year-old in 2026 with $600,000 saved who needs $50,000 a year to cover their lifestyle. If Social Security provides $24,000 a year starting immediately, the portfolio only needs to supply the remaining $26,000 — a withdrawal rate of about 4.3% on the $600,000 balance, close to the traditional benchmark. If that same retiree instead waits three years to claim, drawing the full $50,000 from savings alone during that gap, the withdrawal rate during those three years rises to over 8%, before dropping once a larger Social Security check (closer to $30,000 a year after the delay) takes over most of the load. Whether the bridge strategy or the immediate-claim strategy lasts longer depends heavily on investment returns during those bridge years — illustrating again why a single "safe withdrawal rate" number rarely tells the whole story on its own.</p>'
            . '<p>This guide is educational and not personalized financial advice. A retirement income plan should reflect your own savings, expected expenses, health, and risk tolerance — a financial planner can help translate these general principles into a specific plan for your situation.</p>'
            . '<h2>Building your own withdrawal plan, step by step</h2><ol><li><strong>Add up guaranteed income first.</strong> Social Security, any pension, and annuity income — this is money you do not need to generate from savings.</li><li><strong>Subtract that from your target spending.</strong> The gap is what your portfolio actually needs to produce each year.</li><li><strong>Divide that gap by your starting balance</strong> to see your real first-year withdrawal rate, and compare it with the 4% benchmark in the table above.</li><li><strong>Decide how you will adjust in a down year.</strong> A plan that only works if markets cooperate every single year is a fragile plan — decide in advance whether you would trim discretionary spending after a bad year.</li><li><strong>Revisit the whole plan every few years</strong>, not just once at retirement, as your actual portfolio performance and spending needs become clearer.</li></ol>'
            . '<h2>Sources</h2><p>Social Security Administration <a href="https://www.ssa.gov/benefits/retirement/planner/delayret.html" rel="noopener" target="_blank">guide to delayed retirement credits</a>; William Bengen\'s original 1994 withdrawal-rate research and the widely cited 1998 Trinity study on sustainable retirement withdrawal rates, both long-standing benchmarks in retirement-planning research rather than government figures.</p>'
            . '{faq}',
        'faqs'       => [
            [ 'How long will $500,000 last in retirement?', 'It depends heavily on the withdrawal rate. At a 4% first-year withdrawal ($20,000, rising with inflation), $500,000 has historically had a high probability of lasting 30 years or more. At a 6% withdrawal rate ($30,000), the same balance can run out in roughly 20 to 25 years, depending on investment returns.' ],
            [ 'What is a safe withdrawal rate in retirement?', 'The traditional benchmark is 4% of the starting balance in the first year, adjusted for inflation each year after. Some retirement researchers now suggest a more conservative 3% to 3.5% for very long retirements or more cautious return assumptions, since the 4% figure comes from historical U.S. market data, not a guarantee.' ],
            [ 'Does the 4% rule still work today?', 'It remains a widely used starting benchmark, but many planners treat it as a rough guideline to adjust from rather than a fixed rule — especially for early retirees facing a retirement that could last 40 years or longer, where a lower starting withdrawal rate is often recommended.' ],
            [ 'What is sequence-of-returns risk?', 'It is the risk that poor investment returns early in retirement, combined with ongoing withdrawals, deplete a portfolio much faster than its long-run average return would suggest. Two retirees with the same average annual return over 30 years can end up with very different outcomes purely because of when, in that 30-year span, the good years and the bad years happened to occur.' ],
            [ 'How can I make my retirement savings last longer?', 'Lowering your withdrawal rate has the biggest single effect. Delaying Social Security to grow your guaranteed benefit, staying flexible with spending after down market years, and keeping some growth-oriented investments rather than moving entirely to cash all help extend how long savings last.' ],
            [ 'How much does delaying Social Security really help?', 'The Social Security Administration adds roughly 8% to your monthly benefit for each year you delay claiming past full retirement age, up to age 70. That larger, guaranteed, inflation-adjusted check directly reduces how much you need to withdraw from savings every year for the rest of your life, which can meaningfully extend how long a portfolio lasts even if you draw it down faster during the delay itself.' ],
        ],
    ],
    ];
}
